5:["$","$Le",null,{"slots":{"title":"Writing","subtitle":"Some writings on topics that are close to my heart"},"articles":[{"slug":"bridging","meta":{"title":"Bridging the Gap - Women in Tech","date":"10/03/2025","description":"From a persistent pay gap - women earn 85 cents to a man’s dollar in the US and face a 17% gap in Latvia - to the glass ceiling limiting leadership roles (women hold just 29.2% of STEM jobs), inequalities in tech are real. Causes include societal norms, workplace bias, and lack of STEM encouragement for girls, compounded by imposter syndrome and “Stockholm syndrome” in toxic cultures. Yet, trailblazers like Gwynne Shotwell, Charu Jain, and Latvia’s Anna Andersone inspire us. To bridge the gap, we need fair pay, mentorship, and systemic change. Looking back to Ada Lovelace, I’m motivated to keep pushing - smart, strong women hold up the world, and together, we’ll reshape tech.","tags":["Women in Tech","Software Engineering","STEM","Coding"],"image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/vdeksne/portfolio-images-2025/refs/heads/main/girls.jpg","readingTime":"10"}},{"slug":"cybersecurity","meta":{"title":"The Future of AI in Cybersecurity - Predictions for the Next Decade","date":"20/03/2025","description":"Explore how AI will transform cybersecurity by 2035 with autonomous defense systems and quantum AI. Stats show the AI cybersecurity market hitting $60.6B by 2030, driven by $10.5T in cybercrime costs. A young dev predicts self-patching tools, quantum firewalls, and AI-powered hacker traps - plus the risks of smarter attacks.","tags":["Cybersecurity","Software Engineering","STEM"],"image":"https://github.com/vdeksne/portfolio-images-2025/blob/main/security.png?raw=true","readingTime":"5"}},{"slug":"software","meta":{"title":"My Game-Changer with a Positive Twist (and a Latvian Spark)","date":"10/02/2025","description":"In this upbeat blog, I brainstorm a two-year software project blending impact and Latvian flair. As an AI, blockchain, and cybersecurity fan, I’m eyeing a Vue.js freelancer platform or blockchain amber tracker. I’ll factor in problem-solving, feasibility, passion, innovation, user-friendly design, scalability, local context, and metrics. Inspired by trends like AI fraud detection, I’ll start with an MVP—maybe a phishing tool—then scale up for investors. Latvia’s geopolitical edge pushes me toward security ideas. It’s gonna be a positive win!","tags":["Latvia","Software Engineering","STEM"],"image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/vdeksne/portfolio-images-2025/refs/heads/main/app.png","readingTime":"5"}},{"slug":"tech-hub","meta":{"title":"What Makes a Place Perfect for Software Engineers?","date":"10/01/2025","description":"This blog post is my deep dive into the best places to live and work as a software engineer, straight from the perspective of a Latvian coder. I’ve scoped out 11 awesome spots - Riga, Berlin, Tallinn, Austin, Lisbon, Amsterdam, Stockholm, Copenhagen, Oslo, Milan, and Barcelona—breaking down safety, economy, salary, health, and visa perks (EU citizenship rocks!). Whether I’m chasing big paychecks, chill vibes, or a mix of both, each city’s got its own flavor. It’s my guide to finding the perfect coding haven—bugs beware!","tags":["Latvia","Software Engineering","STEM","TechHub"],"image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/vdeksne/portfolio-images-2025/refs/heads/main/traveler.png","readingTime":"10"}}]}]
